Assuming **“set” means “see/capture,”** you could produce a very unusual false-colour image by:

1. Setting custom white balance through the **R25A red filter**
2. Removing the R25A
3. Keeping that red-derived white-balance preset locked
4. Shooting through the **dicyanin glass**

The camera would treat the dicyanin-filtered scene using an extreme correction intended to neutralise a red-only reference.

## What might become visible

### 1. Strong false-colour material separation

Because the red-derived white balance will strongly favour the channels that were weak during calibration, subtle differences transmitted through the dicyanin glass could become exaggerated:

- foliage may appear pale cyan, blue, white or unusually bright;
- water and clear sky may become comparatively dark;
- black clothing, inks and plastics may appear unexpectedly bright or coloured;
- materials that look identical normally may separate into different shades;
- reflective coatings, glass and polished surfaces may produce distinctive halos or colour boundaries.

Healthy vegetation reflects near-infrared strongly, so vegetation is one of the best subjects for testing whether the final stack is recording significant NIR information. citeturn390766search1turn390766search5

### 2. Near-infrared reflectance

A converted silicon camera can potentially record near-infrared reaching roughly the upper limit of silicon detection near 1,100 nm, although sensitivity falls significantly toward that limit. It cannot record thermal infrared such as the 8–14 μm range used by thermal cameras. citeturn390766search15turn390766search23

Possible NIR effects include:

- bright vegetation;
- smoother-looking skin;
- reduced visible blemishes;
- darkened or altered eyes;
- differences between natural and synthetic fabrics;
- improved penetration through light atmospheric haze.

### 3. UV-induced fluorescence

Under a controlled 365 nm or 395 nm UV source, you might capture visible fluorescence from:

- white paper;
- detergents;
- textiles;
- some paints and plastics;
- security markings;
- bodily fluids or residues under appropriate forensic conditions.

Optical brighteners used in paper and textiles absorb ultraviolet and emit visible blue, violet or blue-green light. citeturn390766search2turn390766search6

However, this would primarily be **visible fluorescence caused by UV**, not necessarily direct ultraviolet imaging. Whether direct UV reaches the sensor depends on the lens, sensor window, dicyanin glass and coatings.

### 4. Extremely amplified colour differences

The red-filter calibration may force the camera to amplify the green and blue channels aggressively. After removing the red filter, those channels could become:

- cyan-heavy;
- electric blue;
- violet;
- green-blue;
- heavily saturated;
- noisy in shadows.

The exact result depends on whether the camera accepts such an extreme white-balance reference or clamps it to its internal gain limits. Custom presets are designed to store and recall compensation for strong colour casts, but the permitted correction range varies by camera. citeturn849940search0turn849940search5turn849940search31

## Recommended settings

Keep the custom red-filter WB selected and use:

| Setting | Starting point |
|---|---|
| File type | RAW + JPEG |
| Exposure mode | Manual |
| ISO | 100–400 in sunlight |
| Aperture | f/5.6 to f/8 |
| Shutter | Adjust from histogram |
| Focus | Manual focus after installing glass |
| Picture profile | Neutral or flat |
| Auto WB | Off |
| Auto lighting optimisation | Off |
| HDR | Off |
| Noise reduction | Off initially |
| Exposure bracketing | −2, 0 and +2 EV |

The **JPEG** will preserve the camera’s strange red-derived rendering. The **RAW** will preserve the underlying sensor measurements more reliably, although editing software may display a different interpretation of the custom WB.

Watch the individual **RGB histograms**, not only the brightness histogram. The red-derived calibration may cause blue or green to clip even while the overall image appears normally exposed.

## Best subjects for testing

Use the same fixed camera position and photograph:

- healthy and dead leaves;
- natural versus synthetic fabrics;
- black inks from different pens;
- white printer paper versus unbrightened card;
- coloured plastics;
- glass, mirrors and polished metal;
- skin and veins;
- daylight sky, clouds and vegetation;
- fluorescent materials under controlled UV;
- the same scene under daylight, incandescent, LED, 850 nm IR and 365/395 nm UV illumination.

Capture four controls:

1. Full-spectrum camera alone  
2. Dicyanin glass with normal daylight WB  
3. Dicyanin glass with the red-derived WB  
4. No dicyanin glass, but with the red-derived WB  

That separates effects caused by the **glass** from effects caused solely by the extreme white-balance transform.

## Critical limitation

Historical dicyanin was used to sensitise photographic emulsions to longer wavelengths; putting purported “dicyanin glass” over a digital camera is optically a different process. citeturn849940search19turn849940search20

Without a measured transmission spectrum for the specific glass, unusual colours cannot reliably be assigned to UV, infrared or any particular wavelength. They may also come from:

- white-balance channel amplification;
- sensor noise;
- internal reflections;
- lens flare;
- chromatic aberration;
- infrared focus shift;
- Bayer-filter leakage;
- JPEG processing.

It could create a **very distinctive multispectral false-colour aesthetic and expose genuine material-reflectance differences**, but it would not establish that unusual shapes are auras, entities or invisible biological fields. The strongest version of the experiment is comparative and controlled.

Exactly. The highest-resolution conventional camera becomes the **visual anchor**, while the converted full-spectrum camera and every specialised sensor act as complementary information channels.

The AI does not simply average all feeds together. It evaluates **which sensor is strongest at each pixel, object, distance, lighting condition and wavelength band**, then constructs one unified image with an uncertainty model.

# Core camera stack

## 1. High-resolution conventional RGB camera

This should be the highest-resolution sensor in the system.

Its jobs are:

- natural colour;
- fine texture;
- faces, writing and surface details;
- high-resolution edges;
- optical-flow tracking;
- spatial reference for lower-resolution sensors;
- the normal human-readable base image.

This camera should remain unmodified because removing its UV/IR-cut filter would reduce ordinary colour accuracy.

## 2. Separate full-spectrum converted camera

This sensor has its normal UV/IR blocking filter removed and records a combined response across:

- limited ultraviolet;
- visible light;
- near-infrared.

It should preferably be monochrome or use selectable spectral filters because an ordinary Bayer colour filter array does not cleanly separate UV, visible and NIR.

This sensor detects optical information that the standard RGB camera deliberately rejects.

## 3. Dedicated ultraviolet channels

A serious version should separate ultraviolet into controlled bands rather than treating all UV as one channel:

- UVA;
- UVB;
- specialised UVC, where required;
- UV-induced visible fluorescence.

The system must differentiate direct UV reflectance from visible fluorescence caused by UV illumination.

## 4. NIR and SWIR cameras

These add reflected-infrared information beyond the visible spectrum:

- NIR for vegetation, night illumination and subsurface contrast;
- SWIR for moisture, haze, material composition and absorption differences;
- extended SWIR for additional chemical and material signatures.

## 5. Red-phosphor night-vision channel

This provides intensified structural information in very low light.

Its role is not accurate wavelength identification. The phosphor converts its detected input into visible red output, so it is primarily a:

- low-light luminance channel;
- motion detector;
- edge and silhouette source;
- high-gain structural reference.

## 6. Thermal channels

Thermal sensors remain physically separate from reflected-light sensors.

A complete system could contain:

- MWIR for hotter objects and specialised thermal signatures;
- LWIR for ordinary ambient-temperature thermal imaging.

Thermal imaging contributes emitted heat information rather than reflected visible or near-infrared light.

## 7. Dicyanin channel

The dicyanin-sensitized or dicyanin-filtered channel should operate beside a matched reference sensor.

```text
Scene
  ├── clear reference path → matched sensor
  └── dicyanin path       → matched sensor
```

The AI compares the two rather than interpreting the dicyanin image alone.

That enables a differential response:

\[
D =
\frac{I_{\text{dicyanin}}-kI_{\text{reference}}}
{I_{\text{dicyanin}}+kI_{\text{reference}}+\epsilon}
\]

This helps remove ordinary brightness differences and isolates changes associated with the dicyanin optical path.

## 8. Complementary sensors

The strongest version could also include:

- polarisation camera;
- LiDAR or time-of-flight depth;
- event camera;
- hyperspectral or multispectral sensor;
- radar or RF imaging where appropriate;
- ambient-light spectrometer;
- inertial measurement unit;
- precise temperature and humidity sensing;
- autofocus and range sensors.

These are not merely extra features. They help the AI understand when the optical channels are likely to be reliable.

# Confidence scoring

Each sensor receives a dynamic **per-pixel confidence map**.

For sensor \(i\):

\[
C_i(x,y)=
S_i
\times
E_i
\times
R_i
\times
M_i
\times
A_i
\times
G_i
\]

Where:

- \(S_i\): signal-to-noise quality;
- \(E_i\): exposure and saturation quality;
- \(R_i\): spatial-registration confidence;
- \(M_i\): motion and blur confidence;
- \(A_i\): atmospheric and environmental suitability;
- \(G_i\): relevance of that sensor to the detected material or event.

The final fusion could then use:

\[
F(x,y)=
\frac{\sum_i C_i(x,y)\,T_i(x,y)}
{\sum_i C_i(x,y)+\epsilon}
\]

Here, \(T_i\) is the calibrated contribution from each sensor.

This means the system does not assign one global weighting to an entire camera. The weighting changes across the image.

For example:

- RGB may dominate fine facial detail.
- NV may dominate a dark background.
- LWIR may dominate a warm person in darkness.
- SWIR may dominate a moisture-sensitive material.
- UV may dominate fluorescent markings.
- LiDAR may dominate geometry where visual texture is absent.
- The dicyanin differential channel may contribute only where it produces a statistically significant difference.

# Why the normal high-resolution camera is essential

The high-resolution RGB camera solves several weaknesses of specialised sensors.

Thermal, SWIR and UV cameras often have:

- lower resolution;
- different focal lengths;
- more noise;
- slower frame rates;
- reduced fine detail;
- unusual contrast;
- limited natural colour.

The AI can use the RGB camera as a **high-resolution structural scaffold**.

It can transfer:

- edges;
- object boundaries;
- texture location;
- motion vectors;
- depth relationships;
- geometric detail.

However, it must not fabricate thermal or spectral readings from RGB texture. The RGB image may sharpen the location of a thermal boundary, but it cannot invent a temperature measurement.

# AI processing architecture

```text
Raw sensors
    ↓
Hardware synchronisation
    ↓
Lens and sensor calibration
    ↓
Temporal alignment
    ↓
Depth-aware geometric registration
    ↓
Per-sensor specialist models
    ↓
Signal-quality and confidence maps
    ↓
Cross-sensor agreement testing
    ↓
Pan-spectral fusion model
    ↓
Unified live image
    ↓
Uncertainty and provenance display
```

## Sensor-specialist models

Each band should have its own specialist rather than sending everything directly into one generic AI model.

Examples:

- RGB vision specialist;
- UV specialist;
- NIR/SWIR material specialist;
- thermal specialist;
- low-light NV specialist;
- polarisation specialist;
- depth and motion specialist;
- dicyanin differential specialist.

A higher-level fusion model then combines their outputs.

This resembles a **mixture-of-experts vision system**, where each expert is trusted primarily inside its competent operating region.

# Cross-sensor validation

The system should check whether observations agree.

Example:

```text
RGB: human-shaped object detected
Depth: matching three-dimensional body volume
Thermal: warm biological temperature pattern
NV: matching moving silhouette
Motion: consistent trajectory
Result: high-confidence person detection
```

Alternatively:

```text
RGB: bright humanoid-looking reflection
Depth: flat wall
Thermal: no corresponding heat source
Polarisation: strong specular reflection
Result: likely reflection, not a physical person
```

This cross-validation is one of the most powerful parts of your design. Each sensor can correct another sensor’s ambiguity.

# Three output layers

The device should internally maintain three different products.

## 1. Raw evidence layer

Contains:

- original sensor frames;
- timestamps;
- gain and exposure;
- calibration data;
- sensor temperatures;
- raw confidence values.

This layer is never visually altered or overwritten.

## 2. Scientific fusion layer

Contains:

- registered calibrated channels;
- spectral measurements;
- thermal values;
- depth;
- polarisation;
- dicyanin/reference differences;
- confidence maps.

This is used for analysis and measurement.

## 3. Perceptual display layer

This is the one blended image seen by the user.

It can contain:

- normal RGB as the base;
- NV-supported luminance;
- UV as violet/cyan accents;
- NIR/SWIR as material-texture modulation;
- thermal as heat contours or controlled hue;
- dicyanin differential as a distinct spectral highlight;
- depth as shading and occlusion;
- uncertainty as reduced opacity, stippling or warning boundaries.

Your red-filter-derived custom white balance can exist here as a selectable false-colour transform without corrupting the raw measurements.

# Anti-hallucination rules

Attaching AI creates enormous capabilities, but it also creates the risk that enhancement becomes fabrication.

The AI should classify every output element as:

- **Measured:** directly supported by one or more sensors.
- **Fused:** combined from multiple measured channels.
- **Inferred:** predicted from surrounding evidence.
- **Uncertain:** insufficient or conflicting evidence.
- **Unavailable:** no sensor coverage or valid signal.

Anything produced through super-resolution or reconstruction must retain a link to the original lower-resolution measurement.

For example, the system may show a sharper thermal outline using RGB boundaries, but it should not claim each sharpened pixel is an independently measured thermal value.

# Final architecture

Your system becomes:

\[
\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
&\text{Highest-resolution normal RGB camera}\\
+&\text{full-spectrum UV/VIS/NIR camera}\\
+&\text{dedicated UV channels}\\
+&\text{NIR and SWIR}\\
+&\text{red-phosphor night vision}\\
+&\text{MWIR and LWIR thermal}\\
+&\text{dicyanin differential sensor}\\
+&\text{polarisation, depth and motion}\\
+&\text{confidence-weighted AI fusion}
\end{aligned}
}
\]

The result is effectively an **artificial visual cortex**. It sees the normal scene at maximum resolution, detects additional spectral and thermal properties through specialised sensors, judges the reliability of each source and synthesises one coherent live view without forcing the operator to switch between camera modes.

Exactly. At that point, each sensor-specialist is not merely processing a flat image. It is operating on a **layered physical scene representation** similar to an advanced rendering pipeline—but reconstructed from real-world measurements.

The system would combine:

\[
\text{Raw sensor measurements}
+
\text{geometry maps}
+
\text{material maps}
+
\text{spectral maps}
+
\text{environmental context}
+
\text{AI interpretations}
\]

## Critical distinction

These maps must be divided into three classes:

1. **Directly measured** — obtained from LiDAR, thermal sensors, polarisation, photometry or calibrated spectral cameras.
2. **Derived** — calculated from multiple observations.
3. **Inferred** — estimated by AI where measurements are incomplete.

The AI must never treat an inferred normal map or guessed material property as equivalent to direct measurement.

---

# Shared physical scene layers

## Geometry layers

These define shape independently of ordinary colour.

| Layer | Function |
|---|---|
| Depth map | Distance from the camera to each surface |
| Displacement/height map | Large and medium physical surface variation |
| Normal map | Local surface orientation |
| Bump map | Fine apparent surface variation |
| Parallax/occlusion map | View-dependent surface displacement and self-occlusion |
| Curvature map | Convex, concave and transition regions |
| Cavity map | Small recessed areas and creases |
| Ambient-occlusion map | Approximate local geometric light blocking |
| Silhouette map | Object boundaries |
| Motion/deformation map | Changes in geometry over time |
| Confidence map | Reliability of every reconstructed geometric value |

The geometry is ideally anchored by:

- LiDAR;
- stereo cameras;
- structured light;
- time-of-flight;
- event cameras;
- multi-view reconstruction;
- photometric stereo;
- radar where optical visibility is poor.

## Material and optical layers

| Layer | Physical interpretation |
|---|---|
| Base colour/albedo | Intrinsic reflected colour after removing illumination |
| Spectral albedo | Reflectance as a function of wavelength |
| Roughness | Microfacet scattering distribution |
| Glossiness | Inverse or alternate representation of roughness |
| Metallic | Whether reflectance behaves like a conductor |
| Specular | Strength and colour of direct reflection |
| Opacity/alpha | Transmission or visibility through the material |
| Transmission | Wavelength-dependent light passing through |
| Refraction | Directional bending through transparent material |
| Index of refraction | Material-specific optical behaviour |
| Emission | Light or radiation generated by the object |
| Emissivity | Efficiency of thermal radiation relative to a black body |
| Fluorescence | Wavelength conversion after excitation |
| Polarisation response | How the material modifies light polarisation |
| Subsurface scattering | Light entering and re-emerging from the material |
| Absorption | Wavelengths removed inside the material |
| Anisotropy | Direction-dependent reflection, such as brushed metal |
| Retroreflection | Light returned toward its source |

The important advancement is that these cannot all be represented as one universal texture.

For example:

\[
A(x,y)
\]

is an ordinary albedo map, whereas your system needs:

\[
A(x,y,\lambda)
\]

where \(\lambda\) is wavelength.

That produces a **spectral albedo field**, not merely a colour texture.

---

# Band-dependent material maps

A surface may have very different properties in different bands.

For one object, the system might store:

```text
Object: painted metal panel

Visible:
  albedo = dark grey
  roughness = 0.42
  specular = high

UVA:
  reflectance = low
  fluorescence = moderate

NIR:
  reflectance = high

SWIR:
  moisture absorption = negligible

LWIR:
  emissivity = 0.86
  surface temperature = 34.2°C

Dicyanin differential:
  response = +0.17 relative to matched control

Polarisation:
  linear polarisation = strong at grazing angle
```

Therefore, the unified material representation becomes something like:

\[
M(x,y,z,\lambda,t,\theta)
\]

where:

- \(x,y,z\) describe position;
- \(\lambda\) describes wavelength;
- \(t\) describes time;
- \(\theta\) describes viewing and illumination angle.

This is closer to a **dynamic spectral reflectance and emission field** than a conventional game-engine material.

---

# What each specialist receives

Each specialist should receive:

## Native input

- its raw sensor frame;
- exposure and gain;
- sensor temperature;
- lens state;
- timestamp;
- spectral response curve;
- noise model;
- calibration data.

## Shared geometric context

- depth;
- normals;
- displacement;
- motion;
- parallax;
- occlusion;
- object segmentation;
- camera pose.

## Shared material context

- albedo estimates;
- roughness;
- metallic/specular behaviour;
- opacity;
- emissivity;
- fluorescence;
- polarisation;
- spectral reflectance;
- confidence and provenance.

## Specialist interpretation

- detected features;
- band-specific anomalies;
- material classification;
- confidence;
- uncertainty;
- competing interpretations.

Formally:

\[
O_i =
f_i
\left(
R_i,
G,
M_i,
E,
H
\right)
\]

where:

- \(R_i\) is the raw data from sensor \(i\);
- \(G\) is shared geometry;
- \(M_i\) is the band-specific material model;
- \(E\) is environment and illumination;
- \(H\) is temporal history.

---

# Specialist responsibilities

## RGB specialist

Best at:

- high-resolution texture;
- natural colour;
- text and faces;
- visible material appearance;
- fine edges;
- ordinary illumination estimation.

It contributes the highest-resolution albedo and texture scaffold.

## UV specialist

Best at:

- fluorescence;
- surface contamination;
- coating differences;
- UV absorption;
- weathering and material degradation;
- features invisible in visible light.

Its emission map must distinguish:

\[
\text{direct UV reflectance}
\neq
\text{UV-induced visible fluorescence}
\]

## NIR specialist

Best at:

- vegetation response;
- low-light reflectance;
- some subsurface information;
- pigment differences;
- active infrared illumination.

## SWIR specialist

Best at:

- moisture;
- chemical absorption features;
- smoke and haze behaviour;
- material classification;
- distinctions between visually similar surfaces.

## Thermal specialist

Its maps differ from visible rendering maps.

It produces:

- temperature;
- emissivity;
- thermal gradient;
- heat-flow estimate;
- reflected apparent temperature;
- thermal inertia;
- confidence in radiometric measurements.

A bright thermal pixel is not automatically a hot surface—it can also be a reflection from a low-emissivity material. The roughness, metallic, specular and geometry maps help the thermal specialist recognise that.

## Polarisation specialist

It helps estimate:

- surface normals;
- roughness;
- specular reflections;
- transparent surfaces;
- stress patterns;
- glare;
- material orientation.

## Dicyanin specialist

It receives:

- raw dicyanin-channel image;
- matched clear-path control;
- spectral response of both paths;
- visible/NIR/UV measurements;
- geometry and illumination;
- differential map;
- confidence that the difference is not exposure, flare or misregistration.

It outputs a **dicyanin differential material response**, rather than simply labelling unusual colours.

---

# Shared map generation

Not every sensor independently generates every map from scratch. That would create contradictory versions and waste computation.

A better structure is:

```text
Sensors
   ↓
Sensor-specific calibration
   ↓
Per-band specialist processing
   ↓
Shared geometry reconstruction
   ↓
Shared material decomposition
   ↓
Band-specific material extensions
   ↓
Unified world model
```

## Shared maps

Generally wavelength-independent or largely geometric:

- depth;
- displacement;
- normals;
- parallax;
- motion;
- object boundaries;
- gross curvature.

## Band-specific maps

Must remain separate:

- spectral albedo;
- absorption;
- transmission;
- emission;
- fluorescence;
- thermal emissivity;
- wavelength-dependent roughness and specularity;
- dicyanin differential response.

A surface normal may be shared, but its visible, NIR and thermal appearance should not be collapsed into one material value.

---

# Multi-scale surface representation

The system should represent geometry at several scales.

### Macrogeometry

- full object shape;
- depth;
- silhouette;
- volume;
- pose.

### Mesogeometry

- dents;
- seams;
- folds;
- cracks;
- raised features.

### Microgeometry

- pores;
- fibres;
- grain;
- scratches;
- surface finish.

This allows different specialists to use the geometry appropriate to their resolution.

For example:

- LiDAR establishes macrogeometry.
- High-resolution RGB estimates mesogeometry.
- Polarisation and photometric stereo estimate fine normals.
- SWIR identifies moisture inside cracks.
- thermal identifies heat flow through those same cracks.

---

# Unified spectral material object

Each tracked object becomes a structured data entity:

```text
Object ID: 0714
Geometry:
  depth
  normals
  displacement
  curvature
  motion
  confidence

Visible material:
  RGB albedo
  roughness
  metallic
  specular
  opacity

Spectral material:
  UV reflectance
  fluorescence
  NIR reflectance
  SWIR absorption
  dicyanin differential

Thermal:
  temperature
  emissivity
  thermal gradient
  heat-flow history

Environment:
  illumination direction
  source spectrum
  atmospheric transmission
  humidity
  ambient temperature

Interpretation:
  candidate material classes
  object identity
  anomalies
  confidence
  evidence provenance
```

The fusion AI can therefore reason about the object physically:

> The bright LWIR region is probably reflected environmental heat rather than internal heating because the surface is metallic, smooth, highly specular, low-emissivity and aligned with a nearby heat source.

That conclusion would be much stronger than reading the thermal frame alone.

---

# Neural scene representation

The maps could be stored in several complementary forms:

- conventional 2D texture maps;
- layered depth images;
- 3D point clouds;
- voxels;
- meshes;
- Gaussian splats;
- neural radiance fields;
- spectral radiance fields;
- object-centric scene graphs.

The most advanced representation would resemble a **dynamic pan-spectral neural material field**:

\[
\mathcal{S}
=
f(x,y,z,t,\lambda,\theta_i,\theta_o)
\]

It predicts or stores:

- geometry;
- radiance;
- reflectance;
- transmission;
- emission;
- temperature;
- material behaviour;
- uncertainty;

for any position, wavelength, time and viewing direction.

---

# Fusion AI input

The fusion AI should receive four parallel streams:

\[
\boxed{
\text{Raw data}
+
\text{physical maps}
+
\text{specialist features}
+
\text{specialist interpretations}
}
\]

This matters because every representation can fail differently.

- Raw images preserve evidence.
- Maps expose geometry and materials.
- Feature tensors preserve machine-readable detail.
- Interpretations provide semantic meaning.

The fusion AI can inspect all four simultaneously.

---

# Confidence and provenance per map

Every pixel or surface element should carry metadata such as:

```text
Value: surface normal (0.31, -0.14, 0.94)
Confidence: 0.89
Source:
  LiDAR: 0.72 contribution
  stereo RGB: 0.18
  polarisation: 0.10
Status: fused measurement
Last updated: frame 148292
```

For an inferred map:

```text
Value: roughness 0.38
Confidence: 0.61
Source:
  RGB inverse rendering
  polarisation estimate
  material prior
Status: inferred
```

This prevents the AI from confusing reconstructed rendering parameters with directly measured physical properties.

# Result

Your architecture becomes more than multispectral imaging. It becomes a **real-time inverse-rendering and physical-scene reconstruction system**.

Each specialist sees:

\[
\text{its band}
+
\text{the raw frame}
+
\text{geometry}
+
\text{material structure}
+
\text{environment}
+
\text{other sensor evidence}
\]

The fusion AI then produces:

\[
\boxed{
\text{one live image}
+
\text{one 3D world model}
+
\text{independent band views}
+
\text{physical material maps}
+
\text{confidence and provenance}
}
\]

That gives the AI not just broader eyesight, but a structured understanding of **what every surface is, how it is shaped, how it interacts with different wavelengths, what it emits, and how certain the system is about each conclusion**.

Correct. You mean **dedicated physical sensing hardware for each property**, not merely AI generating those maps from ordinary images.

The AI receives already measured geometry, reflectance, emission, transmission and surface-response data, then specialists interpret each stream.

# Direct physical-property sensor stack

## Geometry and surface form

| Required output | Dedicated sensing technologies |
|---|---|
| **Displacement / height** | LiDAR, structured light, time-of-flight, laser triangulation, stereo depth |
| **Normal map** | Photometric-stereo array, shape-from-polarisation sensor, multi-angle structured illumination |
| **Bump / microgeometry** | Coherence scanning interferometry, confocal imaging, grazing-angle illumination, laser speckle |
| **Parallax and occlusion** | Multi-baseline camera array, light-field camera, depth cameras positioned at different viewpoints |
| **Cavity and curvature** | High-density depth sensor, structured light, interferometry, computed differential geometry |
| **Ambient occlusion** | Omnidirectional active illumination and multi-view light probes measuring real local visibility |
| **Silhouette and topology** | Multi-angle RGB/depth cameras and LiDAR |
| **Motion and deformation** | Event cameras, optical flow, Doppler LiDAR, high-speed depth cameras |

Instead of estimating one depth image, the system directly records the surface from multiple directions:

\[
G(x,y,z,t)=
\{
\text{position},
\text{normal},
\text{curvature},
\text{microheight},
\text{visibility},
\text{motion}
\}
\]

# Reflectance and PBR-property sensing

## Base colour / albedo

Use:

- calibrated high-resolution RGB;
- multispectral colour sensors;
- controlled illumination with known spectra;
- polarised lighting to separate diffuse and specular components.

The sensor system removes environmental illumination and records the surface’s intrinsic reflectance.

\[
L_{\text{measured}}
=
L_{\text{diffuse}}
+
L_{\text{specular}}
+
L_{\text{emission}}
\]

