# Smart averaging

A simple arithmetic mean would be inadequate because poor simulations should not influence the answer as strongly as good simulations.

Instead, each simulation receives a likelihood or fitness score:

\[
w_i =
P(\text{real measurements}\mid\theta_i)
\]

where:

- \(\theta_i\) is the candidate material-property set;
- \(w_i\) is how well it explains the evidence.

The estimated material property becomes a weighted result:

\[
\hat{\theta}
=
\frac{\sum_i w_i\theta_i}
{\sum_i w_i}
\]

A candidate that poorly matches the real-world data might receive almost zero weight. A candidate matching thermal, mechanical, electromagnetic and acoustic observations receives high weight.

So instead of:

> “Average all 100,000 answers.”

the system performs:

> “Compare all 100,000 candidate worlds against reality, heavily weight the ones that behave correctly, reject inconsistent models, and quantify the remaining uncertainty.”

