The sensor stack observes an unknown object and constructs an observed behavioural fingerprint:

\[
O =
\{
O_{\text{spectral}},
O_{\text{thermal}},
O_{\text{mechanical}},
O_{\text{electrical}},
O_{\text{RF}},
O_{\text{chemical}},
O_{\text{acoustic}}
\}
\]

The AI compares that fingerprint against every material map.

\[
P(m\mid O)
\propto
P(O\mid \mathcal{B}_{m})P(m)
\]

In plain terms:

> How probable are these observations if the unknown material were material \(m\)?

Example:

```text
Observed unknown surface

Optical:
Silver-grey, strongly specular

Electrical:
Highly conductive

Magnetic:
Non-ferromagnetic

Thermal:
Rapid heat spreading

Acoustic:
Longitudinal-wave speed consistent with light metal

Spectroscopy:
Aluminium-compatible oxide and reflectance features

Structural:
Elastic response near 69 GPa

Candidate matches
1. Aluminium 6061-T6 — 93%
2. Aluminium 6082-T6 — 88%
3. Aluminium 7075-T6 — 74%
4. Magnesium alloy — 31%
5. Metallised polymer — 4%
```

The AI could then select a targeted test that best separates the remaining candidates.

> “6061 and 6082 remain difficult to distinguish. Measure conductivity and damping under a controlled thermal cycle.”

# Identification by behaviour, not merely appearance

This would allow material inference even when:

- two materials have the same colour;
- a surface has been painted;
- a polymer has a metallic coating;
- an alloy has aged or corroded;
- the material is a composite;
- environmental conditions distort one sensor;
- spectroscopy alone is inconclusive.

The AI combines evidence from independent physical behaviours.

A material becomes identifiable by its **multiphysics signature**:

\[
\boxed{
\text{what it reflects}
+
\text{what it absorbs}
+
\text{how it deforms}
+
\text{how it conducts}
+
\text{how it heats}
+
\text{how it reacts}
+
\text{how it ages}
}
\]

# Composites and layered materials

The system should not assume every region is one homogeneous material.

It must support:

```text
Object surface
├── acrylic paint
├── corrosion layer
