## Lithography sensor stack

### Optical and spectral sensors

These would measure:

- exposure wavelength and spectral purity;
- illumination uniformity;
- source intensity and dose;
- lens or mirror aberrations;
- flare and scattered light;
- resist absorption and fluorescence;
- film thickness;
- surface contamination;
- reflected spectra from patterned structures;
- critical dimensions through scatterometry;
- overlay between successive layers.

Critical-dimension and overlay metrology are already fundamental semiconductor measurements: CD determines whether features meet their size targets, while overlay measures whether successive patterned layers are correctly aligned. citeturn741618search3turn741618search9

### High-resolution inspection

A combination of optical, hyperspectral and electron-beam inspection could detect:

- missing or bridged lines;
- line-edge roughness;
- collapsed resist structures;
- particles;
- pinholes;
- stochastic EUV defects;
- local critical-dimension variation;
- mask defects;
- etch residue;
- pattern deformation.

ASML’s current inspection ecosystem already combines optical metrology with e-beam inspection for overlay, critical dimensions and individual defect localisation. Your proposal would connect those sensor families through a shared AI world model rather than keeping them as mostly separate tools. citeturn741618search1turn741618search0

Hyperspectral semiconductor inspection is also technically relevant: research systems have demonstrated simultaneous spatial and spectral measurements over approximately 350–1,100 nm for high-throughput wafer analysis. citeturn741618search22turn741618search29

### Geometry and surface sensors

Your PBR-style sensing concept translates well into lithography metrology:

| Your map | Lithography equivalent |
|---|---|
| Height/displacement | Resist and etched-feature height |
| Normal map | Sidewall and surface orientation |
| Bump map | Nanoscale surface roughness |
| Curvature | Rounded corners and line deformation |
| Parallax/occlusion | High-aspect-ratio feature visibility |
| Base colour/albedo | Wavelength-dependent reflectance |
| Roughness | Line-edge, line-width and surface roughness |
| Opacity | Film absorption and transmission |
| Emission | Resist fluorescence, plasma or thermal emission |
| Material map | Resist, substrate, hard mask, metal and dielectric identification |

Through-focus optical techniques can already extract nanoscale three-dimensional differences from ordinary optical microscope data, demonstrating how defocus and optical response can become a sensitive 3D metrology signal. citeturn741618search24

## RF, terahertz and electromagnetic sensing

Your RF-to-THz fabric could contribute measurements that conventional wafer cameras do not directly provide:

- dielectric permittivity of films;
- conductivity and sheet resistance;
- carrier behaviour;
- moisture or solvent remaining in resist;
- layer thickness and internal interfaces;
- plasma state during etching or deposition;
- electromagnetic interference around stages and actuators;
- electrical continuity after patterning;
- resonant responses of fabricated RF structures.

The RF and THz sensors would not replace optical or e-beam metrology. They would provide **electrical and dielectric condition maps** that could be registered with the optical pattern.

For example:

```text
Pattern region 8A42

Optical:
Line width within tolerance

Height:
Normal

THz dielectric response:
Abnormal local loss

Electrical test:
Elevated resistance

Fusion interpretation:
Probable incomplete material conversion or internal film defect
```

